-- EXPECT-ERROR: module `Main` imports `Vault` which requires `Cap(IO.Mut)`, but `IO.Mut` is not declared in `needs`
-- Capabilities widening, Task 2 -- Check 4 (transitive `use`), the REJECT
-- side. Companion to accept/t49 with the `needs IO.Mut` line removed.
-- `Vault` (`stdlib/vault.march:26`) declares `needs IO.Mut`; `Main` `use`s
-- `Vault` AND REFERENCES `Vault.new`, which requires `IO.Mut`, but declares no
-- `needs` of its own, so `declared_needs = []` covers nothing and Check 4
-- raises an ERROR (not a warning -- contrast Check 1b/1c, which are
-- warning-only for direct body/extern uses; Check 4 is a true error just like
-- Check 1 and Check 5).  See core-march-types.md 2.8.6.
--
-- The reference is load-bearing as of 2026-08-06: Check 4 propagation is
-- DEMAND-DRIVEN, so an importer inherits only the capabilities of the
-- functions it actually references.  This fixture's body used to be a bare
-- `()` with no reference at all, which now (correctly) costs nothing --
-- importing a module for one pure function must not drag in its impure
-- siblings' capabilities.  Without the reference this file would silently
-- stop testing Check 4 at all.
mod Main do
  use Vault

  fn main() do
    let _ = Vault.new("t")
    ()
  end
end
